    10	     all in one of four directions and on distinct perpendicular offsets.
    11	   - The fused rule changes three linked stages at once:
    12	       1. reference choice: use the unique shortest segment;
    13	       2. length action: only cut longer segments, never extend anything;
    14	       3. framing: crop the final aligned segments to their minimal bounding box.
    15	   - Small trace, horizontal/end-anchored case:
    16	       input lengths by offset = [8, 3, 6], all share the same right endpoint.
    17	       base code would pick the median-offset row, align every row to that row's span, keep 16x16,
    18	       and could extend or trim depending on the median length.
    19	       combo code picks the 3-cell row as reference, shifts every segment so its start matches the
    20	       short row's start, trims the 8-cell and 6-cell rows to length 3, and finally crops away the
    21	       surrounding background. The visible result is a compact 3-column stripe block.
    22	   - Because the reference is already the minimum length, "clip only" becomes a strict one-way
    23	     contraction: every non-reference segment is longer and therefore gets shortened.
    24	
    25	b) Complexity calibration
    26	   - The generator keeps the original direction set, odd segment counts, irregular offset gaps, and
    27	     1-3 colors so the task still looks like the same puzzle family.
    28	   - To keep the D1 change visible, the shortest segment is forced to be off the median offset line.
    29	     Otherwise many samples would accidentally agree with the original positional reference rule.
    30	   - To keep the D8 change visible, the aligned output layout must sit at least one cell away from
    31	     every canvas border, guaranteeing that tight crop reduces the 16x16 frame.
    32	   - Freedom is limited to direction, segment count, anchor side, shortest-line offset, shortest
    33	     length, and the longer lengths. That is enough variation without making the relation noisy.
    34	
    35	c) Hidden assumptions reviewed against main()
    36	   - Direction detection still assumes same-color adjacency is uniquely strongest along one of the
    37	     four candidate directions. The generator preserves that by drawing only proper separated runs.
    38	   - Segment extraction assumes each segment has a unique predecessor-free start on its offset line.
    39	     Distinct offsets and straight runs preserve this.
    40	   - The original median-offset reference assumption is invalid under the combo and is replaced with
    41	     a new assumption: exactly one segment has minimum length. The generator enforces uniqueness.
    42	   - The original redraw loop always drew target_len cells. Under the combo this is valid only after
    43	     replacing that with min(original_len, target_len); with a shortest reference this simplifies to
    44	     trimming longer lines while leaving the reference unchanged.
    45	   - Tight crop also breaks the old `out = g.copy()` habit because stale foreground inside the crop
    46	     would survive. The combo solver therefore redraws onto a clean background canvas and crops from
    47	     the transformed cells only.
    48	
    49	d) Placement legality
    50	   - Generation is output-first. It first chooses the compact aligned target span, then only accepts
    51	     placements where that span is in bounds for every offset and has an outer margin for cropping.
    52	   - Input segments are derived by lengthening away from a shared start or shared end anchor. Every
    53	     non-reference segment must be strictly longer than the shortest reference, so no extension is
    54	     ever needed in the target transformation.
    55	   - Perpendicular offsets remain distinct, with diagonal gaps >= 4, so segments never overlap or
    56	     visually fuse after alignment.
    57	
    58	e) Visual observability
    59	   - A human sees one uniquely shortest line in the input, then sees all output lines contract to
    60	     that short span and the canvas shrink around them. Those are visible geometric cues.
    61	   - The shortest line changes position across samples and is explicitly decoupled from the median
    62	     offset, so the puzzle does not collapse back to the source rule.
    63	
    64	f) Visual cleanliness
    65	   - No extra markers are introduced. The scene remains only parallel segments on plain background.
    66	   - Irregular spacing keeps tracks visually separate, and tight crop produces a compact stripe block
    67	     rather than a cluttered arrangement.
    68	
    69	g) Color inference
    70	   - Colors are preserved as identities of segments. No color ordering, recoloring, or hidden
    71	     color-dependent rule is introduced, so the inference burden stays geometric.
    72	
    73	Post-change solver assumptions guaranteed by generate_input()
    74	   - There is at least one multi-cell segment and all extracted segments share one dominant direction.
    75	   - All segment line coordinates are distinct, and diagonal spacing is large enough for clean tracing.
    76	   - Exactly one segment is shortest, and it is not on the median perpendicular offset.
    77	   - Every other segment is strictly longer than the reference, so the one-sided trim rule is active.
    78	   - The transformed layout has a nontrivial outer margin, so tight crop changes the output size.
    79	"""
    80	
    81	from pathlib import Path
    82	import random
    83	import sys
    84	
    85	import numpy as np
    86	
    87	_HERE = Path(__file__).resolve()
    88	for _parent in _HERE.parents:
    89	    _arc_utils = _parent / "solvers" / "arc_utils.py"
    90	    if _arc_utils.exists():
    91	        _solvers_dir = str(_parent / "solvers")
    92	        if _solvers_dir not in sys.path:
    93	            sys.path.insert(0, _solvers_dir)
    94	        break
    95	
    96	from arc_utils import get_bg
    97	
    98	
    99	_DIR_CANDIDATES = [(0, 1), (1, 0), (1, 1), (1, -1)]  # horiz, vert, diag \, diag /
   100	
   101	
   102	def _pick_main_dir(g: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
   103	    counts = []
   104	    h, w = g.shape
   105	    for dr, dc in _DIR_CANDIDATES:
   106	        cnt = 0
